The generation pipeline requires an initially convergent and electrically feasible network. The base case must satisfy the following minimum requirements:

• convergent power flow;

• constant-PQ loads;

• connected topology with all in-service loads energized;

• explicit active and reactive power limits for generator and external grid;

• controllable devices consistent with the benchmark action space, with explicit operating bounds where applicable.

Since standard test cases do not always satisfy these requirements, each network undergoes a networkspecific augmentation that defines the missing operating and control bounds without modifying the topology, component availability, or introducing new controllable devices. After this step, the same dataset-generation pipeline is applied across all networks.

Tool-bearing agents interact with the benchmark exclusively through 4 agent-facing tools. The interface allows an agent to inspect the current grid, check whether a proposed maneuver is admissible, evaluate one maneuver, or request a limited empirical ranking of candidate maneuvers. Internal diagnostic routines are never directly callable by the agent.

The four tools are:

• get_grid_topology: inspect the current network state and controllable elements;

• get_action_applicability: check whether one proposed maneuver is admissible;

• run_ac_pf: evaluate one maneuver with the AC power flow solver;

• rank_candidate_maneuvers: evaluate and rank a limited subset of legal maneuvers.
